Regression tests for "fix ablate ... mode deposit"
==================================================

Deposition grows an implicit surface into the gas, the inverse of ablation.
A growing surface runs into gas particles.  The implementation reflects those
particles off the advancing front instead of deleting them, and accounts for
the few that the front genuinely buries.  These tests assert that directly.

Run with:

  cd sparta/src
  make serial
  cd ../tools/testing/deposition
  python3 run_tests.py

Optionally point at a different binary, or run in parallel:

  python3 run_tests.py --exe /path/to/spa_serial
  python3 run_tests.py --exe ../../../src/spa_mpi --ranks 4

Tests
-----

in.test.conserve
  A growing surface in a sealed, reflective box with no inflow or outflow.
  The only way a particle can leave is for the front to bury it, so

      np(start) - np(end)  ==  nburied

  must hold exactly, and the buried mass must equal nburied times the species
  mass.  Run over a sweep of growth rates, from slow enough that nothing is
  buried up to fast enough that some particles are, and at several Nevery
  values.  fix grid/check runs with the error setting throughout, so
  completing the run also proves no particle was left inside the surface.

in.test.momentum
  A growing surface in a fully PERIODIC box, so no domain boundary can take
  momentum from the gas and gas-gas collisions conserve it internally.  The
  surface is then the only place gas momentum can go, and

      [ P_gas(end) - P_gas(0) ] + P_surf + P_buried + P_reflect  ==  0

  must close, where the three surface terms are the momentum handed to the
  surface by ordinary collisions, by particles it buried, and by the
  reflections that push enclosed particles back into the flow.  The residual
  is round-off over roughly a million collisions, about 1e-8 relative.

in.test.momentum.3d
  The same ledger in three dimensions, which exercises marching cubes and the
  triangle intersection rather than marching squares and the line one.

in.test.wall
  A depositing surface advances by accretion, not by motion of its material,
  so the atoms a molecule strikes are at rest: the front velocity decides
  when and where a collision happens but must not appear in the reflected
  velocity.  A wall physically translating at the same speed would be a
  piston and would heat the gas.  Off a stationary plane specular reflection
  preserves each particle's speed exactly, so with a specular wall the gas
  temperature must not move at all as the growth rate is raised.  Raising it
  from 0 to 0.1 raises the surface collision rate by more than half and
  leaves the temperature bit identical.

in.test.guard
  The per-cell csurfs collision lists are rebuilt only when the isosurface is
  regenerated.  A front travelling more than half a grid cell between
  regenerations would reach particles that are never tested against it, so
  fix ablate must abort instead of silently overtaking them.  This input asks
  for such a rate and is expected to fail with an error naming the advance.

in.test.ablate
  The same setup with the default mode = ablate.  A receding surface never
  engulfs anything, so both deposition counters must stay at zero, confirming
  the advancing-front code is inert when deposition is not in use.

What the counters mean
----------------------

  f_ablate      summed corner point values, i.e. how much material exists
  f_ablate[3]   nburied, particles incorporated into the film
  f_ablate[4]   buried mass
  f_ablate[8]   nfrontreflect, salvage reflections after a regeneration

nfrontreflect counts particles the regenerated isosurface enclosed and that
were pushed back into the flow.  Every one of those would have been deleted
by a delete-on-engulf implementation, so the count is a direct measure of what
this feature saves.  It grows with the growth rate and with Nevery, since a
larger jump per regeneration leaves more residual work for the safety net.

Not covered here
----------------

  - axisymmetry; see examples/explicit2implicit/in.deposit.axi.spherecone,
    which runs under fix grid/check with the error setting
  - Kokkos, whose particle move is a separate implementation and does not
    read the refreshed geometry, so growth is resolved once per Nevery there
